  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nRidging through the column will provide a trend toward mostly clear \nskies and light winds tonight. This may allow for patchy fog/stratus \ndevelopment overnight into early Monday, especially over ne IN and \nnw OH where lows will likely dip below xover values. \n\nReturn flow and associated strong WAA on the backside of the exiting \nridge will set the stage for breezy ssw winds and highs into the 80s \nMonday into Tuesday. A low amplitude, mid level impulse has \nconsistently been modeled to track east through the Upper Midwest \nand central Great Lakes Monday night into Tuesday morning. \nAssociated theta-e advection and slight graze of mid level ascent \nsupport retaining low chances (10-30%) for a few showers/storms in \nthe far north during this time.  \n\nA west to east oriented frontal zone tied to the aforementioned \nshortwave sags south into the area later Tuesday afternoon into \nWednesday. An additional surge of deeper moisture and several upper \nlevel waves ejecting east out of an Intermountain West upper low \nlikely activates the front with increasing rain/embedded thunder \nchances. Highest PoPs (60-70%) favor areas south of the US 30 \ncorridor. Cooler, yet, still above normal temps otherwise into \nWednesday. \n\nEnsemble and deterministic model solutions continue to differ on \nwhether a stronger storm system develops into the Great Lakes in \nresponse to potential phasing (or not) of southern and northern \nstream waves Thursday into Friday. This brings inherent uncertainty \nregarding precip chances and the magnitude of winds and CAA behind a \ncold front Friday into Saturday.",
